<article class="env-lemma" id="0BID">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BID">Lemma <span data-tag="0BID">9.8.10</span>.</a> Let $E/F$ be a finite or more generally an algebraic extension of fields. Any subring $F \subset R \subset E$ is a field. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\alpha \in R$ be nonzero. Then $1, \alpha , \alpha ^2, \ldots $ are contained in $R$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/09GG" data-tag="09GG">9.8.5</a> we find a nontrivial relation $a_0 + a_1 \alpha + \ldots + a_ d \alpha ^ d = 0$ with $a_ i \in F$. We may assume $a_0 \not= 0$ because if not we can divide the relation by $\alpha $ to decrease $d$. Then we see that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  a_0 = \alpha (- a_1 - \ldots - a_ d \alpha ^{d - 1})  \]
</div>
    <p>
       which proves that the inverse of $\alpha $ is the element $a_0^{-1} (- a_1 - \ldots - a_ d \alpha ^{d - 1})$ of $R$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>